WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.100
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, episode 124.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.680
It's July 7th, 2026.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.460
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.680
Euclid telescope has found the two oldest quasars known.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.800
Their light crossing more than 13 billion years to reach us.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.980
NASA's Parker Solar Probe detected charged dust grains

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:26.360
riding magnetic plasma waves inside the corona during close passes.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.340
The grains interact with waves that appear

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.820
to transfer energy into the surrounding plasma.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.440
Wave amplitudes and grain sizes were recorded

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.940
at multiple perihelia, allowing direct comparison

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.760
with models of coronal heating.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.660
Researchers are modeling how the dust wave coupling scales

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.220
with distance from the sun.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.020
Follow-up passes at lower altitudes will test whether

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.860
the mechanism holds closer in.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.580
Parker Solar Probe measurements show charged dust grains

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:59.660
being carried on magnetic plasma waves inside the sun's corona.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.080
The interaction appears to channel energy into the surrounding plasma,

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:07.840
offering one concrete pathway that could explain temperatures

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.620
millions of degrees hotter than the visible surface.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.120
Wave amplitudes and grain sizes were recorded

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.380
during the spacecraft's closest approaches,

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.040
allowing direct comparison with models.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.380
Follow-up passes at even lower perihelia

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.920
will test whether the same coupling persists closer to the sun.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.960
If the sun were scaled to the size of a basketball

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.080
sitting in downtown Toronto, its corona would extend outward

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.600
past the CN tower yet remain almost completely invisible

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:37.340
to the eye.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.960
Dust grains roughly a micrometer across somehow

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.340
persist inside this million-degree plasma long enough

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.740
to be observed by Parker Solar Probe.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.600
The grains are swept along by alpha waves whose magnetic

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.820
tension acts like an invisible rail,

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.100
shielding the particles from immediate vaporization.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:02.820
At 6.9 solar radii, the probe recorded wave speeds near 200 kilometers per second,

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.280
while the grains themselves moved at a few tens of kilometers per second relative

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.800
to the wave crests.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.860
The exact balance between radiative cooling,

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.500
collisional heating, and magnetic confinement is still unknown.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.780
Models cannot yet predict how far inward the grains can travel

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.100
before they are destroyed.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.520
From one probe revealing hidden solar processes,

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.760
we turn to an object that arrived from an entirely different star system.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.720
Comet 3i slash Atlas, the third confirmed interstellar object,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:38.300
shows unusual volatile ratios compared with solar system comets.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:43.460
Spectra from multiple NASA facilities reveal its composition and trajectory.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.960
Those measurements trace material from another star,

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.020
system formed during the universe's peak star formation era.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:57.420
Continued tracking will reveal how the object's surface evolves as it exits the inner solar system.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.040
Archival searches are underway for additional interstellar visitors.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:04.860
While one visitor from afar is studied,

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:08.880
Earth itself hosted two close asteroid passes this weekend.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.780
Two separate asteroid flybyes occurred over the weekend,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.880
each passing within a few lunar distances of Earth.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:21.820
Ground-based observatories tracked the objects using both radar and optical telescopes.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.380
The data refined their orbits and physical properties.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.080
The events highlight the growing catalog of near-Earth asteroids monitored

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.860
for potential future encounters.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.980
Future campaigns will focus on similar passes expected later this year.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:42.900
From natural visitors, we shift to the rocket that sent dozens of human-made ones into orbit.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:47.120
A Falcon 9 lifted off from Vandenberg Space Force Base,

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.900
carrying 81 small satellites into sun-synchronous orbit.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:56.140
Deployment lasted roughly two and a half hours and included fire detection sensors,

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:59.780
military technology demonstrators, and 3D printers.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:04.920
The first stage landed on a drone ship in the Pacific after separation.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:09.480
Several payloads remain unclaimed by public owners at this time.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.200
Follow-on missions in the Transporter Series are already manifested.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.040
While SpaceX flew another ride share,

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.680
a German startup secured its first North American launch site.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:27.680
Izar Aerospace signed an agreement to develop a launch pad for its spectrum rocket on Canadian territory.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:33.100
The deal is tied to a separate submarine contract between Canada and Germany.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:39.020
Spectrum is designed as a small lift vehicle for both commercial and government customers.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:44.280
Site preparation is expected to begin once regulatory approvals are complete.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:49.160
The facility would mark Izar's first North American operational base.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.100
From new launch infrastructure on the ground,

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.100
we move to a satellite preparing for its own ride to geostationary orbit.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:00.980
The second media set, third-generation imager satellite,

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:07.400
weighing 3,800 kilograms is undergoing final checks ahead of an Arian 6 flight.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.260
Once in orbit, it will deliver higher resolution imagery

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.860
and faster updates for European and North African forecasts.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:20.000
Its infrared channels will improve detection of rapidly developing storms.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.400
Journalists may apply by 16 July to attend the launch in French Guiana.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.340
Data validation will continue for several months after arrival on station.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.540
From forecasting Earth's weather,

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:37.140
we look at long-duration tests for crews heading to the Moon and Mars.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:43.260
Applications are now open for crews willing to live inside a closed habitat for 12 months.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:49.160
The study will test medical monitoring, food systems, and psychological support protocols.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:54.460
Selected participants will simulate surface operations without leaving the facility.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:06:00.060
Results are intended to inform both Artemis lunar stays and eventual Mars missions.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:06.140
Deadlines and eligibility criteria are posted on the agency's Human Research website.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:11.940
While future crews train on the ground, one Canadian astronaut who already flew to the Moon

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:18.260
is stepping away. Jeremy Hanson, who flew aboard Artemis II, is stepping down from active duty

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:24.120
at the Canadian Space Agency. His departure follows a career that included training for lunar

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:30.320
missions and public outreach across Canada. The agency has not yet named a replacement for his

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:35.780
flight assigned role. Hanson's experience will remain available through advisory positions.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:40.360
Future Canadian astronaut selections are expected later this decade.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:46.900
From one Canadian leaving orbit, we turn to simple naked eye sights visible before dawn this week.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:53.060
The last quarter moon passes roughly seven degrees north of Saturn in the early morning sky.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:58.280
Neptune lies nearby and can be spotted with binoculars under dark conditions.

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:04.240
Observers are also encouraged to look for the coma star cluster in the same region.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:09.460
The geometry changes quickly each morning as the moon continues its orbit.

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:14.320
Charts and timing details appear in the magazine's weekly observing column.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:20.640
While planets in the moon offer easy targets, a distant galaxy shows intricate structure worth

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:28.620
imaging. The Bard spiral NGC 7098 lies about 100 million light years away in Octans.

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:34.060
High resolution imaging from Chile reveals a bright inner ring surrounded by a

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:38.620
fainter outer ring and tightly wound spiral arms wrapped around the central bar.

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:44.280
The structure is thought to result from dynamical resonances within the galaxy's disc.

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:49.780
Amateur and professional observers continue to monitor the system for transient events.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.040
The image appears in the magazine's current picture of the day series.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:08:00.760
From one striking galaxy image, we move to a set of cosmic scenes colored red, white, and blue.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:07.580
Four new composite views combine Chandra X-ray data with optical and infrared observations to

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:14.100
produce red, white, and blue cosmic scenes. Three of the images include sonified versions that

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:20.480
translate X-ray brightness into audible tones. The release pairs with broader NASA efforts linking

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:26.360
a Apollo heritage to the Artemis program. Viewers can explore the full set on the Chandra mission

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:33.080
site. Additional data products will appear in coming weeks. While telescopes capture striking views,

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:39.960
astronauts aboard the ISS documented another kind of landmark. Astronauts captured nighttime images

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:45.540
of several stadiums and urban areas preparing for the upcoming 2026 World Cup.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:52.160
The views from 250 miles up show city lights and infrastructure changes visible from orbit.

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:58.220
The photo series continues a long tradition of documenting major sporting events from the station.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:04.940
Images are being released through NASA's Earth Observatory. Additional passes over host regions

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:11.080
are planned through the summer. From Earth observation, we turn to experiments running right now inside

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:18.420
the station. Expedition 74 crew members started experiments examining cartilage tissue engineering and

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:24.780
cardiovascular responses in microgravity. The work uses specialized incubators and monitoring

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:31.400
equipment already aboard the outpost. Parallel maintenance tasks on space suits and cargo transfers

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:37.420
continued throughout the day. Data from these investigations will feed into long-term health protocols

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:42.960
for future missions. Weekly science summaries are posted on the NASA Space Station blog.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:49.280
From current station science, we look at concepts that could change how crews move on the lunar or

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:55.720
Martian surface. A University of Michigan team designed pressurized, actuated tunnels that would

00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:03.160
link habitats to rovers and science stations. The concept was submitted to NASA's 2026 Moon to Mars

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:09.380
Academic Challenge. Once deployed, the tunnels would eliminate the need for repeated suit-up cycles

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:14.900
during surface traverses. Prototypes are being tested in analog environments on Earth.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:21.260
The team will present further refinements at upcoming NASA workshops. From surface operations,

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:27.740
we close with two small but telling cultural and manufacturing notes. Tiny carvings of legendary

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:33.780
Indian researchers have been placed inside a needle's eye and will fly aboard the Vikram-1 rocket.

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:39.580
The payload demonstrates precision manufacturing techniques suitable for future space artifacts.

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:46.780
The launch is scheduled as part of India's growing small satellite manifest. Post-flight analysis will

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:53.260
assess material durability in the space environment. Similar cultural payloads are under consideration for

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:59.420
subsequent missions. Next time, we will be watching for new results from Parker Solar Probe's next

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:06.060
close approach. That covers today's space and science news. Share this with a fellow space enthusiast

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:11.800
if you found it interesting. I'm Patrick in Vancouver, see you tomorrow, and if you'd rather watch

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:18.560
than listen, find us on YouTube at Nerra Network, links in the show notes. And before you go, this show is

